    "md5": "3c48c0e1241ccf84291f8503f9535978",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1718,
    "chunk_text": "Prime Minister delivers lecture at University of Oxford\n\nPrime Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya attended the 2026 Oxford School of Global and Area Studies (OSGA) Annual Lecture held at the University of Oxford on 19 May, where she delivered a lecture titled \u201cThe Politics of Development: Sri Lanka and Beyond.\u201d\n\nThe lecture, held at the St Antony\u2019s College, University of Oxford, and organised by the OSGA, is the department\u2019s flagship annual event that brings together leading academics, policymakers, and practitioners to discuss contemporary global challenges.\n\nAddressing an audience comprising students, academics, and researchers from across the University of Oxford, Dr. Amarasuriya reflected on the intersections of development, governance, and politics in Sri Lanka while discussing the broader realities faced by countries attempting to achieve meaningful social and economic reform.\n\nDuring her lecture, the Prime Minister emphasised that development cannot be understood only through policies, statistics, and economic models, but must also be viewed through the everyday lived experiences of people. She highlighted that governance often requires balancing ideals with practical realities, particularly in countries recovering from economic crises, institutional challenges, and longstanding inequalities.\n\nDr. Amarasuriya also spoke extensively on women\u2019s political participation and representation in Sri Lanka, noting the growing involvement of women in grassroots political movements and leadership spaces. She reflected on the efforts taken to create greater political opportunities for women and the continuing challenges faced in transforming deeply rooted social and political structures."
  },
